1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,240
The seeds of a revolution are in our own backyard.

2
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:08,060
Gadgets that mulch, vacuum, and turn on a dime.

3
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:11,780
Yard works no chore when mowers push themselves.

4
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:14,220
And sprinklers check the weather report.

5
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:16,600
Don't like your grass?

6
00:00:17,140 --> 00:00:18,320
Just roll out a new lawn.

7
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:19,960
Re-engineer it.

8
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:21,280
Or fake it.

9
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:23,980
Place one call for instant shade.

10
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:26,200
Or even something fishy.

11
00:00:26,201 --> 00:00:29,200
Now, Yard Tech on Modern Marbles.

12
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:54,200
In yards across America, the lawn is a labor of love.

13
00:00:54,201 --> 00:00:59,553
love. The basis of this national grassroots obsession, turfgrass.

14
00:00:59,554 --> 00:01:01,200
Turfgrass is a dense

15
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:08,260
cover of grass and roots in the top layer of the soil. More than 32 million acres of

16
00:01:08,261 --> 00:01:13,505
it are planted in the United States, twice the acreage of cotton.

17
00:01:13,506 --> 00:01:15,200
Some 58 million homes

18
00:01:15,201 --> 00:01:16,570
sport grass lawns.

19
00:01:16,571 --> 00:01:22,200
Grass is typically a permanent fixture, but one unusual lawn was built for

20
00:01:22,201 --> 00:01:23,133
for travel.

21
00:01:23,134 --> 00:01:30,000
The University of Phoenix Stadium is a dome, home to the NFL's Arizona Cardinals.

22
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:32,014
But there's a problem.

23
00:01:32,015 --> 00:01:36,440
Beyond hosting football, it's also booked as a site for events that would

24
00:01:36,441 --> 00:01:38,050
pulverize a grass surface.

25
00:01:38,051 --> 00:01:42,200
They can bring in tractor pulls, big boy toys, I mean, you name it,

26
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:44,567
we've had every kind of show in the world in here.

27
00:01:44,568 --> 00:01:46,820
But you can't do it if you have a field in here.

28
00:01:46,821 --> 00:01:51,700
The solution is a new twist on indoor-outdoor carpet.

29
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:54,880
But this carpet is made of real grass.

30
00:01:56,180 --> 00:01:59,000
As the turf flourishes outside in the Arizona sun,

31
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:03,020
spot watering and computer-controlled irrigation keeps it green.

32
00:02:04,380 --> 00:02:07,220
The field is in one piece, not segmented.

33
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:11,760
So to move it inside, the turf can't be rolled in sections.

34
00:02:11,761 --> 00:02:18,760
Instead, when game time looms, it rolls into the dome in one hulking mass of grass.

35
00:02:18,761 --> 00:02:26,760
The two-acre field sits in a massive tray in layers, much like a birthday cake.

36
00:02:26,761 --> 00:02:31,760
A steel support structure and metal decking support the tray.

37
00:02:31,761 --> 00:02:37,760
The plastic tray sits one inch above the support structure to allow for water drainage below.

38
00:02:37,761 --> 00:02:44,760
drainage below. The turf grows atop a one-foot-thick root zone. This layer is a

39
00:02:44,761 --> 00:02:49,400
mixture of sand and reinforcing fibers that provides optimum stability for the

40
00:02:49,401 --> 00:02:55,880
athletes. Moving this 19 million pound chunk of sod takes surprisingly little

41
00:02:55,881 --> 00:03:03,260
power. An extension cord and a 13-track rail system are all that's needed.

42
00:03:03,261 --> 00:03:10,260
A utility trench allows workers to monitor the move from below.

43
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:19,260
There's our warning signal for the field moving.

44
00:03:19,261 --> 00:03:26,260
Now we get to listen to that for 70 minutes.

45
00:03:26,261 --> 00:03:31,260
Here we're looking at the gearbox to drive the wheel.

46
00:03:31,261 --> 00:03:34,000
Here's a one horsepower motor.

47
00:03:34,001 --> 00:03:38,560
There are 76 one horsepower motors driving this field at this time.

48
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:44,800
We connect it with two electrical cords.

49
00:03:44,801 --> 00:03:48,640
We come halfway in, we stop, unplug the cords,

50
00:03:48,641 --> 00:03:50,180
take them to the other end,

51
00:03:50,181 --> 00:03:53,720
plug them in back down the other end,

52
00:03:53,721 --> 00:03:56,560
and then we'll come in the second half of the way.

53
00:04:01,260 --> 00:04:15,260
For all you homeowners saddled with a lawn that can't move, the real action lies in what

54
00:04:15,580 --> 00:04:26,600
you move on top of it.

55
00:04:26,601 --> 00:04:30,600
from zero to seven miles per hour in seconds flat.

56
00:04:30,601 --> 00:04:33,640
With engines as powerful as 30 horsepower,

57
00:04:33,641 --> 00:04:36,440
the riding lawnmower has been the big dog of the yard

58
00:04:36,441 --> 00:04:37,740
for decades.

59
00:04:37,741 --> 00:04:42,140
In the 1950s, the riding mower was the new status symbol.

60
00:04:42,141 --> 00:04:45,480
It was advertised as a little car.

61
00:04:45,481 --> 00:04:50,560
Some of them had martini glass holders, all kinds of things.

62
00:04:50,561 --> 00:04:52,820
And everybody wanted a riding mower.

63
00:04:52,821 --> 00:04:55,960
They were fun.

64
00:04:55,961 --> 00:04:58,080
Say hello to the new big dog.

65
00:04:58,081 --> 00:05:01,900
It's called ZTR, or zero turn radius.

66
00:05:01,901 --> 00:05:03,660
And it's taking a big bite out of the time

67
00:05:03,661 --> 00:05:05,900
it takes to mow the lawn.

68
00:05:05,901 --> 00:05:07,260
They are more productive.

69
00:05:07,261 --> 00:05:09,080
They get the job done faster.

70
00:05:09,081 --> 00:05:12,820
From a homeowner standpoint, they're more fun to operate.

71
00:05:12,821 --> 00:05:14,700
Part of the fun is trading the steering wheel

72
00:05:14,701 --> 00:05:17,000
for a pair of levers.

73
00:05:17,001 --> 00:05:19,680
The levers control two independent motor drives

74
00:05:19,681 --> 00:05:20,760
that power the rear wheels.

75
00:05:20,761 --> 00:05:28,320
The zero turn is getting a lot of attention these days at lawn and garden centers across

76
00:05:28,321 --> 00:05:29,021
America.

77
00:05:29,780 --> 00:05:31,700
This is a zero turn lawnmower.

78
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:36,320
The basic principle behind it, if you make a right-handed turn, your right-hand transmission

79
00:05:36,321 --> 00:05:38,060
is not going to work at all.

80
00:05:38,180 --> 00:05:40,000
The left-hand transmission does all the work.

81
00:05:42,780 --> 00:05:44,760
If you notice, it turns in place.

82
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:48,740
The right-hand wheel is not moving, but the left-hand wheel is working completely.

83
00:05:48,741 --> 00:05:58,040
You can go into much tighter places and then get back out without lots of extra maneuvering.

84
00:05:58,440 --> 00:06:04,800
Or when you come up to a small tree, you can go around it one time and be done.

85
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:09,340
If a homeowner can go out and do the job in half the time, that's worth a lot.

86
00:06:13,060 --> 00:06:16,000
In the centuries before the ZTR and its brethren,

87
00:06:16,001 --> 00:06:20,180
turning a pasture into a lawn was a tedious and uneven proposition.

88
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:24,160
As 17th century English gardens gained popularity,

89
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:28,680
the owners began to employ small armies of workers wielding scythes.

90
00:06:29,080 --> 00:06:32,120
It was a long stick with a large blade on the end of it.

91
00:06:32,380 --> 00:06:34,840
An individual would swing back and forth,

92
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:37,980
and that's how the lawn or grass area would then be cut.

93
00:06:38,460 --> 00:06:43,880
Very labor-intensive, took a good deal of skill to be able to use that over time,

94
00:06:43,881 --> 00:06:46,520
especially if you're trying to get a low and level cut.

95
00:06:48,080 --> 00:06:51,540
In 1830, English inventor Edwin Budding

96
00:06:51,541 --> 00:06:54,340
envisioned a better way to get a low and level cut.

97
00:06:56,580 --> 00:06:58,940
He built a large cast iron machine

98
00:06:58,941 --> 00:07:01,400
with a revolving cutting cylinder at the front.

99
00:07:02,560 --> 00:07:04,960
He called it a real lawnmower,

100
00:07:05,380 --> 00:07:07,500
and it's a design that lives on today.

101
00:07:08,520 --> 00:07:11,360
A real mower shears grass like a pair of scissors.

102
00:07:11,361 --> 00:07:15,360
A central shaft rotates a series of cutting blades.

103
00:07:15,361 --> 00:07:18,360
The turning blades come into contact with a bed knife.

104
00:07:18,361 --> 00:07:22,360
The scissor action cuts the blades of grass.

105
00:07:22,361 --> 00:07:26,360
For decades, few people saw the need for a lawnmower.

106
00:07:26,361 --> 00:07:32,360
But in the United States, that changed when the urban lawn first came into vogue.

107
00:07:32,361 --> 00:07:39,360
Over 38 patents for real-type mowers were issued between 1868 and 1873.

108
00:07:39,361 --> 00:07:47,360
At the turn of the century, several mower inventions added small steam and even gasoline-fueled

109
00:07:47,361 --> 00:07:50,360
engines.

110
00:07:50,361 --> 00:07:53,360
Many inventors lay claim to the rotary lawnmower.

111
00:07:53,361 --> 00:08:00,360
But in 1931, the Louisville Electric Manufacturing Company built and marketed one of the world's

112
00:08:00,361 --> 00:08:01,360
first.

113
00:08:01,361 --> 00:08:08,360
The rotary design ultimately became the standard by the 1960s.

114
00:08:08,361 --> 00:08:13,560
The rotary mower employs a single blade revolving at around 2,800 revolutions per minute

115
00:08:13,561 --> 00:08:16,680
to cut the grass and then discharge it through a chute.

116
00:08:19,160 --> 00:08:23,460
A mulching mower uses a higher deck without a chute to suspend the grass.

117
00:08:24,620 --> 00:08:27,900
A blade with a longer cutting edge repeatedly chops it to pieces

118
00:08:27,901 --> 00:08:30,860
and returns it as mulch back into the lawn.

119
00:08:32,080 --> 00:08:37,320
It puts the nutrients from the grass blades, the decomposition of the grass blades,

120
00:08:37,321 --> 00:08:41,947
back into the turf. If you're bagging, you don't have to dispose of the clippings.

121
00:08:41,948 --> 00:08:42,648
So

122
00:08:42,649 --> 00:08:48,121
there's the landfill issues and containment issues of grass clippings.

123
00:08:48,122 --> 00:08:49,420
Since 1907, the

124
00:08:49,421 --> 00:08:54,725
Toro Company has been one of the leading lawnmower makers in the United States.

125
00:08:54,726 --> 00:08:55,800
The Toro Testing

126
00:08:55,801 --> 00:09:03,516
and Design Center in Bloomington, Minnesota designs and builds mower prototypes.

127
00:09:03,517 --> 00:09:04,480
The pieces

128
00:09:04,481 --> 00:09:06,740
are laser cut and assembled.

129
00:09:06,741 --> 00:09:11,420
They're then subjected to a battery of tests that can last

130
00:09:11,421 --> 00:09:18,887
for years. The engineers test for safety, performance, and durability.

131
00:09:18,888 --> 00:09:21,980
The standard durability drill

132
00:09:21,981 --> 00:09:23,561
is called the bump test.

133
00:09:23,562 --> 00:09:28,040
Here we try to simulate the entire life of the machine, which may be

134
00:09:28,041 --> 00:09:34,300
3,000 hours, but we try to accelerate the life by having the biggest possible bumps on what we

135
00:09:34,301 --> 00:09:39,820
called a roll dyno, which shocks the machine at a very regular basis, and we try to get

136
00:09:39,821 --> 00:09:44,800
the end result of a light onto the machine in a reasonable period of time, which might

137
00:09:44,801 --> 00:09:46,820
be a month or two months.

138
00:09:50,240 --> 00:09:55,100
Mowers are noisy, so Toro engineers are constantly trying to build a quieter machine.

139
00:09:57,740 --> 00:10:01,060
The sound test tries to pinpoint noise that can be eliminated.

140
00:10:01,061 --> 00:10:08,740
What we're looking for is objectionable sounds or noises that might be coming from the panels that are on the machine,

141
00:10:08,920 --> 00:10:11,500
the engine, the fan, the hydraulics.

142
00:10:13,580 --> 00:10:19,440
While microphones measure external machine noise, a second test judges the effects on the operator's ears.

143
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:25,860
This is the test where we try to measure what the sound level would be at the operator's ear,

144
00:10:25,980 --> 00:10:29,320
and we try to stay within certain measurable limits.

145
00:10:31,060 --> 00:10:38,060
Not all yards are flat, so Toro uses a tilt table test to determine machine rollover angles.

146
00:10:38,061 --> 00:10:39,741
It has two main purposes.

147
00:10:39,742 --> 00:10:46,060
One, to check the parking brake of the machines, and second is to check the overall stability.

148
00:10:46,061 --> 00:10:57,322
We go up to first 16.7 degrees, which is the minimum level for parking brakes, and then we go all the way up to, in this particular case, 25 degrees, which is the

149
00:10:57,323 --> 00:10:59,060
upper limit of stability.

150
00:10:59,061 --> 00:11:04,060
Sometimes mower operators take their eyes off the row.

151
00:11:04,061 --> 00:11:09,060
The drastic stake test is an alarming suburban vision of what could happen.

152
00:11:09,061 --> 00:11:19,060
It's to represent the worst we could imagine a customer doing in their lawn if they hit the water main or a sprinkler head or other objects in the lawn.

153
00:11:19,061 --> 00:11:25,060
A one-inch diameter steel stake is positioned below ground level.

154
00:11:25,061 --> 00:11:27,360
the engineers engage the mower blade,

155
00:11:28,100 --> 00:11:31,000
move it over the impact zone, and then cringe.

156
00:11:33,820 --> 00:11:36,320
And what we're after there is to make sure

157
00:11:36,321 --> 00:11:39,080
that there are no failures on the machine

158
00:11:39,081 --> 00:11:41,920
to make sure that the machine can still be used

159
00:11:41,921 --> 00:11:44,840
for the intended purpose, which is cutting grass.

160
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:49,360
These and dozens of other tests

161
00:11:49,361 --> 00:11:51,600
help engineers build safer and better mowers.

162
00:11:55,060 --> 00:12:02,760
Recently, Toro began testing an autonomous lawnmower with high-precision global positioning and obstacle recognition capabilities.

163
00:12:06,180 --> 00:12:12,300
The prototype represents one possible future option for commercial and municipal grounds and golf courses.

164
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:17,760
But the future is now for the gadget-happy homeowner.

165
00:12:17,761 --> 00:12:26,460
Luis Medina founded his company, Evatec, to build a mower for people who would rather do their yard work sitting down.

166
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:31,080
I used to cut my grass with a regular, typical push mower.

167
00:12:32,140 --> 00:12:37,060
And I wanted to come out with something easier to use, not just for me, but for my friends.

168
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:43,280
The Evatec machine puts mowing at your fingertips with wireless joystick control.

169
00:12:43,281 --> 00:12:47,080
Well, here we have the transmitter.

170
00:12:47,620 --> 00:12:49,560
It has two joysticks that controls a unit.

171
00:12:50,260 --> 00:12:52,760
The transmitter sends the signal to the brain.

172
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:57,540
The brain decodes the signal through a very sophisticated algorithm

173
00:12:57,541 --> 00:13:02,680
that makes the mower go forward, reverse, and zero turning radius.

174
00:13:06,660 --> 00:13:09,760
All that movement is powered by an electric hybrid engine.

175
00:13:13,280 --> 00:13:18,280
A separate gasoline engine operates the cutting blade.

176
00:13:18,281 --> 00:13:23,280
It even comes with its own seed spreader attachment.

177
00:13:23,281 --> 00:13:31,280
Choosing a remote controlled lawn mower over a self-propelled or a ZTR model is a lifestyle choice.

178
00:13:31,281 --> 00:13:36,280
But there are other ways to beautify your lawn.

179
00:13:36,281 --> 00:13:41,280
You can brush it, slice it, or if you need an upgrade,

180
00:13:41,281 --> 00:13:45,840
Upgrade. You can get it cut, rolled, stacked, and delivered.

181
00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:53,340
Inside this greenhouse complex in Marysville, Ohio,

182
00:13:53,900 --> 00:13:57,500
even a snowstorm can't delay the future of the American lawn.

183
00:13:59,600 --> 00:14:02,660
The grass breeders at Scott's Miracle-Gro company

184
00:14:02,661 --> 00:14:05,660
do more than just figure out how to keep your lawn green.

185
00:14:06,820 --> 00:14:10,500
They're conducting experiments aimed at re-engineering the lawn itself.

186
00:14:11,280 --> 00:14:15,280
As public water supplies dwindle and fuel prices rise,

187
00:14:15,281 --> 00:14:20,280
they're trying to breed grasses that need less water and less mowing.

188
00:14:20,281 --> 00:14:25,280
These greenhouse facilities allow us to conduct some of the more controlled and precise experiments

189
00:14:25,281 --> 00:14:27,108
that we do with the Scots Company.

190
00:14:27,109 --> 00:14:30,280
We can control light, we can control the soils that we use,

191
00:14:30,281 --> 00:14:34,280
we can control the irrigation, the nutrition that we're providing,

192
00:14:34,281 --> 00:14:40,280
and really simulate just about any environment that you'd find any place in the world.

193
00:14:40,281 --> 00:14:47,100
The greenhouse contains 12 rooms, each designed to simulate different climates while growing a variety of grasses.

194
00:14:48,760 --> 00:14:54,640
In one room, they're developing slow-growing, or slow-mow grass, to give lawnmowers a break.

195
00:14:56,920 --> 00:15:01,740
These grasses are bred to possess fewer of the hormones that encourage plant cells to elongate.

196
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:08,780
And then they will cross those and go through this plant breeding that can take up to 12 years

197
00:15:08,781 --> 00:15:13,280
to develop a grass that will then give a desirable lawn without requiring a lot of mowing.

198
00:15:16,280 --> 00:15:19,760
Another experiment could put an end to brown lawn syndrome

199
00:15:19,761 --> 00:15:24,720
by crossbreeding popular grass varieties with those that can withstand desert conditions.

200
00:15:26,580 --> 00:15:28,580
These grasses are in a drought test.

201
00:15:29,120 --> 00:15:31,480
They look the same now. They have the same soil.

202
00:15:31,900 --> 00:15:33,880
The difference is we are starting to withhold the water.

203
00:15:34,400 --> 00:15:36,220
After a period of time, some of them will die.

204
00:15:36,221 --> 00:15:40,800
Some of them will just turn brown and maybe green up later when they have water.

205
00:15:41,260 --> 00:15:43,420
The key is we're trying to find the ones that are going to last.

206
00:15:44,720 --> 00:15:48,360
A lawn that requires less water could soon be coming to your neighborhood.

207
00:15:49,180 --> 00:15:50,500
But one thing is certain.

208
00:15:51,140 --> 00:15:53,560
It will look nothing like your ancestors' lawn.

209
00:15:55,960 --> 00:15:58,800
The word lawn dates to 16th century Europe.

210
00:15:59,340 --> 00:16:03,540
And the old English word lawned, meaning open space.

211
00:16:03,541 --> 00:16:10,540
It simply was an area that livestock grazed, and that grazing kept the grass short,

212
00:16:11,060 --> 00:16:15,240
and that short-cropped grass is really the genesis of lawns.

213
00:16:16,500 --> 00:16:19,740
Prior to the 16th century, there were few lawns in Europe.

214
00:16:22,060 --> 00:16:25,900
Pastures and public green spaces contained a smorgasbord of grass varieties.

215
00:16:27,060 --> 00:16:29,920
The grass types were quite different than what we see here.

216
00:16:29,921 --> 00:16:34,620
In fact, some of the grasses, or early lawns rather, were actually things like chamomile

217
00:16:34,621 --> 00:16:37,360
or clover and meadow grasses.

218
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:41,320
And these were things that were native to Northern Europe at the time.

219
00:16:42,680 --> 00:16:47,720
French landscape architect André Le Nôtre designed one of the first ornamental lawns

220
00:16:47,721 --> 00:16:51,560
in 1661 at the Gardens of Versailles near Paris.

221
00:16:55,460 --> 00:16:59,280
Soon after, British noblemen began planting formal English gardens.

222
00:16:59,920 --> 00:17:03,700
But for the English peasant class,

223
00:17:04,180 --> 00:17:06,320
the lawn was still an impractical dream.

224
00:17:07,720 --> 00:17:10,940
The grass wasn't necessarily greener in the American colonies.

225
00:17:12,680 --> 00:17:15,280
Native grasses like broom straw and wild rye

226
00:17:15,281 --> 00:17:18,960
didn't have the nutrients to sustain the grazing animals brought from Europe.

227
00:17:19,400 --> 00:17:23,280
So very early on in the 1600s,

228
00:17:23,480 --> 00:17:25,980
colonists were riding home to Europe

229
00:17:25,981 --> 00:17:29,580
asking for clover and grass seed to be sent.

230
00:17:29,680 --> 00:17:32,280
Many of the colonists' animals starved to death

231
00:17:32,380 --> 00:17:35,720
over the winter before the colonists could get grass seed

232
00:17:35,820 --> 00:17:39,020
that would sustain them.

233
00:17:39,120 --> 00:17:41,420
Even with imported European grass,

234
00:17:41,520 --> 00:17:43,940
the low-cut, English-style lawn didn't germinate

235
00:17:44,040 --> 00:17:48,040
on American soil until after the Civil War.

236
00:17:48,140 --> 00:17:50,240
Grass was for the rich.

237
00:17:50,340 --> 00:17:52,580
Thomas Jefferson was among them.

238
00:17:52,680 --> 00:17:55,980
He grew and nurtured a lawn at Monticello.

239
00:17:55,981 --> 00:17:59,980
The average homeowner didn't plant grass in his yard.

240
00:17:59,981 --> 00:18:02,980
Grass near the home often attracted snakes and rodents,

241
00:18:02,981 --> 00:18:05,980
but more importantly, grass wasn't good eating.

242
00:18:05,981 --> 00:18:08,980
Most people lived on farms,

243
00:18:08,981 --> 00:18:12,980
and around the farmhouse you would have beaten dirt.

244
00:18:12,981 --> 00:18:16,980
You might have a small garden,

245
00:18:16,981 --> 00:18:18,980
vegetable garden, herb garden.

246
00:18:18,981 --> 00:18:22,980
But basically it was a working area.

247
00:18:22,981 --> 00:18:28,920
In the late 1800s, however, the lawn took root, at least in the city.

248
00:18:30,460 --> 00:18:35,660
Mechanical lawnmowers, fertilizer, and access to city water made growing grass affordable.

249
00:18:37,680 --> 00:18:44,180
The lawn also got a boost from the people who depended on lush grass, the United States Golf Association.

250
00:18:45,180 --> 00:18:50,020
Thanks to its research, improved strains of Kentucky bluegrass and other varieties hit the market.

251
00:18:50,021 --> 00:18:55,000
But it took a suburb to spark a yard revolution.

252
00:18:56,120 --> 00:19:01,720
In 1947, Levittown became one of the first suburban tract home developments.

253
00:19:02,720 --> 00:19:06,240
Levittown was built in potato fields in Long Island.

254
00:19:06,660 --> 00:19:07,440
There were no trees.

255
00:19:08,500 --> 00:19:14,140
And the easiest way to cover the dirt and the construction scars was grass.

256
00:19:16,560 --> 00:19:19,980
Soon, middle-class America was obsessed with grass lawns.

257
00:19:20,020 --> 00:19:24,500
Everyone wanted one, and the Scots Company was happy to deliver.

258
00:19:26,120 --> 00:19:29,600
Founded by Civil War veteran O.M. Scott in 1868,

259
00:19:30,180 --> 00:19:34,500
the company learned that the quickest way to a man's heart was through his golf clubs.

260
00:19:35,100 --> 00:19:38,420
They were very early mass marketers.

261
00:19:39,100 --> 00:19:45,080
They bought or obtained the mailing lists for golf clubs,

262
00:19:45,081 --> 00:19:56,400
and they targeted golfers, and they sent out newsletters to tell people how to have a home lawn that looked like a golf course.

263
00:19:58,480 --> 00:20:03,860
Today, golf courses and American lawns are cut from the same roll, with a sod cutter.

264
00:20:04,360 --> 00:20:05,720
Sod is instant lawn.

265
00:20:06,380 --> 00:20:10,260
For those not patient enough to plant it themselves and wait a few weeks for it to develop,

266
00:20:10,261 --> 00:20:12,460
the sod just gives you an automatic lawn.

267
00:20:12,461 --> 00:20:16,440
It's nice and green, and some people prefer that.

268
00:20:17,460 --> 00:20:19,220
Passat Farm operates like a nursery.

269
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:24,980
It grows grass specific to the local climate over a period of 18 months.

270
00:20:26,660 --> 00:20:32,700
A sod cutter skims the turf a quarter of an inch below the surface, creating carpet-like rolls.

271
00:20:34,020 --> 00:20:36,920
The rolls provide a mature lawn in a matter of minutes.

272
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,960
But some homeowners prefer their grass homegrown.

273
00:20:40,961 --> 00:20:45,960
For that, they need pure, straight-from-the-bag grass seed.

274
00:20:45,961 --> 00:20:50,960
Seed is grown and harvested like any other crop.

275
00:20:50,961 --> 00:20:55,960
Because of its cool, wet climate during winter and spring and dry summers,

276
00:20:55,961 --> 00:21:02,960
Oregon is the leading producer, boasting over 1,500 production farms.

277
00:21:02,961 --> 00:21:05,960
The farmers first cut swaths.

278
00:21:05,961 --> 00:21:10,960
Swathing severs the plant's moisture supply and helps it to dry quicker.

279
00:21:10,961 --> 00:21:13,960
Then they use combines to harvest.

280
00:21:13,961 --> 00:21:18,960
The seed is then cleaned and bagged according to very specific formulas.

281
00:21:18,961 --> 00:21:23,960
It's a good idea to have multiple varieties involved in a seed mix.

282
00:21:23,961 --> 00:21:27,960
We try and mix and match a strength and a weakness against each grass

283
00:21:27,961 --> 00:21:30,960
so that if something happens in a lawn, like a disease for example,

284
00:21:30,961 --> 00:21:34,960
it doesn't affect all the varieties, there's something there to take over.

285
00:21:34,961 --> 00:21:39,400
But some homeowners are not purists.

286
00:21:40,460 --> 00:21:43,700
Seeding a yard takes time, and sod can be very costly.

287
00:21:44,480 --> 00:21:48,320
For them, imitation is the sincerest form of ground cover.

288
00:21:49,560 --> 00:21:53,620
This new lawn in Phoenix, Arizona is getting a buff and polish.

289
00:21:54,720 --> 00:21:55,480
But look closer.

290
00:21:56,420 --> 00:21:57,360
The grass is fake.

291
00:21:58,620 --> 00:22:02,600
Synthetic grass, or new grass, is a vast improvement on AstroTurf.

292
00:22:02,601 --> 00:22:07,740
the fake grass fad that carpeted sports fields and yards in the 1970s and 80s.

293
00:22:09,480 --> 00:22:14,560
It was made of polyethylene, an abrasive material with no shock absorption qualities.

294
00:22:16,860 --> 00:22:25,120
With the new grass products versus real grass, in addition to your watering, mowing, fertilizing, thatching,

295
00:22:25,121 --> 00:22:33,120
In new grass, the only maintenance required is an occasional leaf blower, plastic rake, or broom just to remove debris.

296
00:22:34,720 --> 00:22:43,260
Today's synthetic grass, version 2.0, is made of the less abrasive polypropylene with twisted nylon fibers for shock absorption.

297
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:53,060
Customers can even choose their own imitation variety, rye, fescue, or bluegrass, and have it pre-mowed to the height they desire.

298
00:22:53,061 --> 00:23:00,060
Synthetic turf is an alternative to natural grass and a possible answer to shrinking water resources.

299
00:23:00,061 --> 00:23:08,060
But if you're worried about your real lawn sucking up too much water, don't call 1-800-FAKE-GRASS quite yet.

300
00:23:08,061 --> 00:23:13,060
There is a high-tech alternative.

301
00:23:13,061 --> 00:23:18,060
It waters the lawn only when the local weather station gives it permission.

302
00:23:23,060 --> 00:23:31,300
Lurking beneath the turf in millions of American yards is a gadget that dispenses the elixir

303
00:23:31,301 --> 00:23:33,260
crucial to the survival of a lawn.

304
00:23:33,261 --> 00:23:37,480
These days, a lot of people are concerned with drought and how we're using water.

305
00:23:37,481 --> 00:23:38,480
We don't want to waste water.

306
00:23:38,481 --> 00:23:41,880
It's a very precious resource.

307
00:23:41,881 --> 00:23:47,920
In many yards, this resource flows through an underground sprinkler system.

308
00:23:47,921 --> 00:23:53,920
The homeowner programs a computerized controller that executes a watering schedule.

309
00:23:53,921 --> 00:23:58,200
But there's a new computer brain that thinks for itself.

310
00:23:58,201 --> 00:24:02,180
It's a smart controller called ET Manager.

311
00:24:02,181 --> 00:24:07,060
And its only master is the weather itself.

312
00:24:07,061 --> 00:24:14,300
The computer links wirelessly with a nearby weather monitoring station.

313
00:24:14,301 --> 00:24:18,640
The weather station measures temperature, humidity and other data, and then transmits

314
00:24:18,641 --> 00:24:21,860
hourly updates.

315
00:24:21,861 --> 00:24:26,620
The controller receives the data, and then tells the sprinkler system when to water and

316
00:24:26,621 --> 00:24:27,620
for how long.

317
00:24:27,621 --> 00:24:32,800
It actually knows how much water the landscape is using and puts back only the amount of

318
00:24:32,801 --> 00:24:37,720
water that the plants need without any over-watering, without runoff and without waste.

319
00:24:37,721 --> 00:24:39,640
Water savings can be tremendous.

320
00:24:39,641 --> 00:24:44,200
We found you can save as much as 30, even 40 percent on your water bill.

321
00:24:44,201 --> 00:24:48,520
With savings like that, you can bet smart irrigation will eventually become a standard

322
00:24:48,521 --> 00:24:52,480
in the American yard.

323
00:24:52,481 --> 00:24:55,880
We've come a long way from the garden hose.

324
00:24:55,881 --> 00:25:02,320
In the early 1870s, the B.F. Goodrich Company began manufacturing the rubber hose.

325
00:25:02,321 --> 00:25:07,140
It made effective watering of the American lawn possible.

326
00:25:07,141 --> 00:25:12,860
In 1907, along came the first true sprinkler, the Plouviette.

327
00:25:12,861 --> 00:25:15,100
The water drove a turbine.

328
00:25:15,101 --> 00:25:19,700
The turbine distributed the water stream while turning on an axis.

329
00:25:19,701 --> 00:25:22,540
But better designs lay ahead.

330
00:25:22,541 --> 00:25:29,020
In 1935, businessman Clem LaFetra began marketing a sprinkler that was first developed to irrigate

331
00:25:29,021 --> 00:25:32,620
California orange groves.

332
00:25:32,621 --> 00:25:37,060
It was called the Horizontal Action Impact Sprinkler.

333
00:25:37,061 --> 00:25:40,540
It marked the beginning of the Rain Bird Company.

334
00:25:40,541 --> 00:25:42,180
The one I'm holding is a Model 60.

335
00:25:42,320 --> 00:25:46,280
It's the first production model that Ringbird actually made and sold.

336
00:25:46,600 --> 00:25:52,200
It will go 360 degrees, in other words, a full circle, as it's spraying the water.

337
00:25:53,900 --> 00:25:59,260
The impact sprinkler has a spring-loaded spoon arm that repeatedly interrupts the water stream,

338
00:25:59,960 --> 00:26:02,020
creating an evenly spread curtain of water.

339
00:26:02,880 --> 00:26:07,040
As the arm returns, it strikes the sprinkler head, causing it to rotate.

340
00:26:07,041 --> 00:26:17,560
Today's sprinklers go to boot camp at the Rain Bird testing facility in Tucson, Arizona.

341
00:26:17,561 --> 00:26:22,480
On the demo lawn, both rotary sprinklers and circular pop-up sprinklers that have survived

342
00:26:22,481 --> 00:26:29,700
various water torture tests are taking a final exam.

343
00:26:29,701 --> 00:26:36,660
That includes the 115E, one of the world's most powerful gear-driven rotor sprinklers.

344
00:26:36,661 --> 00:26:44,520
It casts a jet of water as far as 115 feet, providing 360 degrees of coverage.

345
00:26:44,521 --> 00:26:49,900
For golf courses or backyard megaloins, just one of these monsters can water an area equivalent

346
00:26:49,901 --> 00:26:56,820
to the size of a football field.

347
00:26:56,821 --> 00:27:01,500
In the test center, Rain Bird's team of hydro engineers puts the sprinkler's crucial head

348
00:27:01,501 --> 00:27:05,940
assemblies through pure hell.

349
00:27:05,941 --> 00:27:08,880
We literally just run them and run them and run them until they break.

350
00:27:09,200 --> 00:27:13,440
We find out why they break, we fix that and continue to run them and make a better product.

351
00:27:15,460 --> 00:27:18,020
Sprinklers can also take a beating in your own backyard.

352
00:27:18,980 --> 00:27:24,180
An accidental water pressure surge called water hammer can occur in a long section of pipe.

353
00:27:27,520 --> 00:27:29,360
The result could be explosive.

354
00:27:29,660 --> 00:27:32,780
The burst test simulates the potential yard disaster.

355
00:27:32,781 --> 00:27:37,360
So we've set up kind of an extreme test out here that can get hundreds of pounds of pressure,

356
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:43,300
400 plus pounds of pressure, and we make sure that our sprinkler heads can withstand that kind of pressure.

357
00:27:45,580 --> 00:27:50,940
Rainbird sprinklers perform under pressure, but can they deliver water to every blade of grass?

358
00:27:51,780 --> 00:27:54,180
This is Rainbird's distribution test.

359
00:27:54,340 --> 00:27:59,920
We have over 1,000 catch cans here, and we're measuring the amount of water in each one.

360
00:27:59,921 --> 00:28:04,920
They're equally spaced apart and we're trying to get a very smooth, even distribution pattern.

361
00:28:04,921 --> 00:28:11,920
Computers measure the amount of water and we can adjust the nozzle technology so that we get a perfect, even distribution.

362
00:28:13,920 --> 00:28:17,920
Distribution is never an issue with drip irrigation.

363
00:28:17,921 --> 00:28:26,920
This water-stingy method is designed for trees, bushes, gardens, flowerbeds, or any other part of the yard except the grass.

364
00:28:26,921 --> 00:28:33,080
It uses what we call spot watering emitters or small area emitters.

365
00:28:33,081 --> 00:28:37,480
It literally drips the water on drop by drop and you run it for much longer periods of

366
00:28:37,481 --> 00:28:42,260
time just at the base of each individual plant.

367
00:28:42,261 --> 00:28:48,800
Irrigation and its evolving technologies are the mandatory accessories for a green lawn.

368
00:28:48,801 --> 00:28:53,720
But one trip to the lawn in Garden Isle reveals a garage-clogging collection of yard gadgets

369
00:28:53,721 --> 00:28:55,320
you may not even know that you need.

370
00:28:57,200 --> 00:29:00,540
The tractor supply company showcases the latest technology

371
00:29:00,541 --> 00:29:02,580
for the do-it-yourself backyard pro.

372
00:29:03,280 --> 00:29:07,920
We've got tillers, lawn tractors, chainsaws, trimmers, blowers,

373
00:29:08,400 --> 00:29:11,040
every gadget you can possibly imagine to use in your yard

374
00:29:11,041 --> 00:29:12,860
and to keep it green all summer long.

375
00:29:14,380 --> 00:29:17,780
One versatile gadget is the yard's version of a Swiss Army knife.

376
00:29:19,080 --> 00:29:21,920
This is the basic line of weed trimmers everybody's used to seeing,

377
00:29:21,921 --> 00:29:24,920
But the newest thing in the yard is the four-cycle trimmer.

378
00:29:24,921 --> 00:29:26,720
It has a highly efficient motor that

379
00:29:26,721 --> 00:29:28,920
keeps the environment clean.

380
00:29:28,921 --> 00:29:30,920
It also has a fast-attached system

381
00:29:30,921 --> 00:29:37,920
where you can put on the regular weed trimmer end, an edger.

382
00:29:37,921 --> 00:29:43,920
You can also use a tree pruner, a hedge trimmer,

383
00:29:43,921 --> 00:29:45,920
and a blower attachment, all with one unit,

384
00:29:45,921 --> 00:29:47,920
and use one motor in your garage as opposed

385
00:29:47,921 --> 00:29:50,920
to having different pieces of equipment.

386
00:29:50,921 --> 00:29:53,920
Detested by many, the ear-splitting leaf blower

387
00:29:54,020 --> 00:29:56,920
is being converted to a kinder, gentler tool.

388
00:29:57,020 --> 00:29:59,920
What we have here is the latest in the backpack blowers.

389
00:30:00,020 --> 00:30:02,920
This has 140-mile-an-hour wind speed, a real low vibration

390
00:30:03,020 --> 00:30:06,920
motor, and a smart start spring will help it easier to start.

391
00:30:07,020 --> 00:30:08,920
A lot less fatigue on you when you don't have all the vibration,

392
00:30:09,020 --> 00:30:11,920
but it does hang off your back and easy controls,

393
00:30:12,020 --> 00:30:14,920
and you can work with it for hours.

394
00:30:15,020 --> 00:30:18,920
The 21st Century Home Store carries an endless catalog

395
00:30:18,921 --> 00:30:25,920
catalog of power gadgets to help the do-it-yourselfer keep order in the yard.

396
00:30:25,921 --> 00:30:31,920
But when it comes to moving a 40-foot oak tree into your backyard, you're going to need

397
00:30:31,921 --> 00:30:35,920
some help.

398
00:30:35,921 --> 00:30:42,920
High above this backyard, a crew is preparing to bring down a monster.

399
00:30:42,921 --> 00:30:48,600
Trees are the pillars that can give a yard scale, but sometimes they must come down.

400
00:30:51,860 --> 00:30:54,380
Bringing a tree down is only half the battle.

401
00:30:55,280 --> 00:30:57,940
The other half is cutting it down to size.

402
00:30:59,800 --> 00:31:03,120
The woodcheper is the Godzilla of yard gadgets.

403
00:31:04,480 --> 00:31:12,900
It's designed with rotating drums or discs and sharp knives that can devour a tree and reduce it to sawdust in a matter of minutes.

404
00:31:12,920 --> 00:31:18,780
bringing down a tree is a relatively quick job

405
00:31:18,781 --> 00:31:22,700
but planting a tree and waiting for a shady canopy

406
00:31:22,701 --> 00:31:25,180
could take 15 or 20 years

407
00:31:25,181 --> 00:31:29,440
for those who can't wait that long

408
00:31:29,441 --> 00:31:33,000
an outfit like the Davy Company can supersize your order

409
00:31:33,001 --> 00:31:37,080
they're a select fraternity of tree movers

410
00:31:37,081 --> 00:31:40,860
the Davy crew hasn't met a tree they can't handle

411
00:31:40,861 --> 00:31:44,300
When the trunk is 24 inches in diameter or larger,

412
00:31:44,860 --> 00:31:46,160
they use a gantry system.

413
00:31:47,500 --> 00:31:50,520
They trench beneath the tree and insert pipes

414
00:31:50,521 --> 00:31:51,980
to act as a lifting platform.

415
00:31:52,900 --> 00:31:55,220
It's a hydraulic process of actually lifting

416
00:31:55,221 --> 00:31:58,400
or jacking the tree up, very similar to what you see

417
00:31:58,401 --> 00:32:01,720
in the building movers or the structural movers

418
00:32:01,721 --> 00:32:03,900
around the country that move buildings.

419
00:32:04,820 --> 00:32:07,000
Once we've got the tree lifted vertically,

420
00:32:07,001 --> 00:32:09,080
we have tracks where we can roll it

421
00:32:09,081 --> 00:32:15,700
or we bring in our trailer that we assemble alongside the tree and then roll it beneath the tree.

422
00:32:17,680 --> 00:32:23,280
If the tree is less than 12 inches in diameter, a customized trailer-mounted spade is all they need.

423
00:32:24,840 --> 00:32:30,100
The hydraulic spade uses a high-pressure pump to drive its blades deep beneath the roots.

424
00:32:31,880 --> 00:32:35,040
One scoop, and this tree is ready to roll.

425
00:32:35,041 --> 00:32:40,080
The Davie Company put down roots in the U.S. in 1880.

426
00:32:41,860 --> 00:32:45,900
By the late 1920s, the company had perfected a moving method.

427
00:32:46,420 --> 00:32:50,700
They used what was called an Irish whip, or some people call it an English spar.

428
00:32:51,060 --> 00:32:55,900
And it was a device where you actually mounted wheels to the tree itself

429
00:32:55,901 --> 00:33:00,200
and tilted it down and towed it to the receiving site

430
00:33:00,201 --> 00:33:02,380
and then stood it back up and planted it.

431
00:33:02,381 --> 00:33:09,380
In this muddy field on a tree farm west of Houston, Texas, the Davie crew is preparing

432
00:33:09,720 --> 00:33:13,743
to move a 40-foot tall, 50,000-pound red oak tree.

433
00:33:13,744 --> 00:33:16,720
This is one of six trees destined for

434
00:33:17,460 --> 00:33:18,730
a single backyard.

435
00:33:18,731 --> 00:33:23,880
Once the crew has excavated the soil around the trunk, trimming the roots

436
00:33:23,881 --> 00:33:25,000
is the biggest concern.

437
00:33:25,001 --> 00:33:29,776
Our initial process is to come in and root prune the tree.

438
00:33:29,777 --> 00:33:32,000
We determined that for this

439
00:33:32,001 --> 00:33:35,680
to have a successful transplant, we sized the root ball at 12-foot diameter.

440
00:33:37,280 --> 00:33:41,040
The root ball is a neat package encased in burlap and steel wire.

441
00:33:43,360 --> 00:33:45,536
It's the tree's life support system.

442
00:33:45,537 --> 00:33:48,800
The crew trims the roots to a safe length based on the

443
00:33:48,801 --> 00:33:52,405
size of the tree and then drives in steel pipes.

444
00:33:52,406 --> 00:33:56,160
Once we have a significant number of pipes beneath

445
00:33:56,161 --> 00:34:00,637
the ball, we'll mount a beam with U-bolts to hold the pipe in a rigid platform.

446
00:34:00,638 --> 00:34:01,600
So essentially we

447
00:34:01,601 --> 00:34:06,000
we have a grid of pipe, which is our steel lifting device.

448
00:34:08,180 --> 00:34:11,160
Rainstorms have turned the earth at this tree farm into thick mud.

449
00:34:13,200 --> 00:34:16,760
The moisture has increased the overall weight by some 5%.

450
00:34:16,761 --> 00:34:22,480
Getting the heavy tree out of the muck is no simple task.

451
00:34:26,500 --> 00:34:31,420
The tightly wound root ball and its support platform will prevent damage during transit.

452
00:34:31,600 --> 00:34:38,780
A tree hurtling down the freeway is officially a wide load and must travel with a police escort.

453
00:34:41,020 --> 00:34:44,200
Upon its arrival in Houston's elegant River Oaks neighborhood,

454
00:34:44,860 --> 00:34:48,520
the team begins the arduous process of maneuvering the trailer into the backyard.

455
00:34:52,520 --> 00:34:54,360
Now it's time for some heavy lifting.

456
00:35:01,600 --> 00:35:06,100
This tree move is called a round ball crane process.

457
00:35:06,101 --> 00:35:10,480
It will use a 120-ton crane to try and lift the 50,000-pound

458
00:35:10,481 --> 00:35:13,920
tree to its new home.

459
00:35:13,921 --> 00:35:15,380
The crane must work in a tight space.

460
00:35:21,520 --> 00:35:27,000
Existing tree limbs and power lines complicate the lift.

461
00:35:27,001 --> 00:35:29,020
We've left the tarp on on purpose

462
00:35:29,021 --> 00:35:33,140
so that as we brush by this existing tree, we don't snag a limb.

463
00:35:33,480 --> 00:35:36,620
Now we're going to pick it up and lift it up and over the house

464
00:35:36,621 --> 00:35:39,060
and set it down in the receiving spot.

465
00:35:41,360 --> 00:35:43,740
The connections are checked and rechecked.

466
00:35:44,060 --> 00:35:47,620
The last thing anyone wants is a 40-foot oak tree falling into the house.

467
00:35:51,160 --> 00:35:54,260
Once the tree is airborne, all eyes are on the root ball.

468
00:35:55,280 --> 00:35:58,220
As you notice, the root ball is the integrity of the tree.

469
00:35:58,221 --> 00:36:03,920
We treat that like a cake, and therefore we don't want to crack the icing on the cake.

470
00:36:04,220 --> 00:36:06,780
That's why we do all of our lifting from the structural bottom.

471
00:36:09,700 --> 00:36:12,400
With the tree dangling a few feet above its new home,

472
00:36:12,920 --> 00:36:16,300
it's spun to find the right spot that will cast shade near the house.

473
00:36:17,140 --> 00:36:20,420
Well, these limbs are longer. Let's let them go out that way.

474
00:36:21,980 --> 00:36:24,600
That way, to that side. Stop, stop there.

475
00:36:26,600 --> 00:36:27,940
The move is complete.

476
00:36:28,220 --> 00:36:29,840
But the work is far from over.

477
00:36:30,440 --> 00:36:37,360
All of the trees that are transplanted essentially are going into a critical care method at that point in time

478
00:36:37,361 --> 00:36:40,760
because we are cutting off a good percentage of the root system.

479
00:36:40,761 --> 00:36:45,440
So we're trying to regenerate new roots once the tree is installed.

480
00:36:46,780 --> 00:36:51,140
The key to root growth is efficient watering and loose aerated soil.

481
00:36:51,141 --> 00:36:57,680
The crew buries perforated drainage pipe on a slope to ensure that gravity will carry

482
00:36:57,681 --> 00:37:00,400
water away from the tree and avoid overwatering.

483
00:37:00,401 --> 00:37:06,140
A backfill of loose soil compost will help with the drainage.

484
00:37:06,141 --> 00:37:12,660
Essentially, it's a two to three year process to regenerate enough of a root system for

485
00:37:12,661 --> 00:37:17,500
the tree to survive on its own without the critical care treatments.

486
00:37:17,501 --> 00:37:24,500
The Davie Company has moving trees down to a science.

487
00:37:24,501 --> 00:37:31,500
But another company aims to transform the plain old backyard into its own ecosystem.

488
00:37:31,501 --> 00:37:38,500
One waterfall, one pond, and one fish at a time.

489
00:37:38,501 --> 00:37:45,500
The American backyard has long been the home turf of man's best friend.

490
00:37:45,501 --> 00:37:50,500
But today, a new creature is challenging the dog's domain.

491
00:37:50,501 --> 00:37:53,500
And you don't even have to clean up after him.

492
00:37:53,501 --> 00:37:57,500
Koi fish originated in China, and in the 19th century,

493
00:37:57,501 --> 00:38:00,500
the Japanese bred them for enhanced color.

494
00:38:00,501 --> 00:38:04,500
They are now tenants of a new vision of the American yard,

495
00:38:04,501 --> 00:38:07,500
the waterscape.

496
00:38:07,501 --> 00:38:11,500
This living, breathing pond is a suburban wilderness adventure

497
00:38:11,501 --> 00:38:14,500
designed to bring out the child in all of us.

498
00:38:14,501 --> 00:38:21,500
Pacific Outdoor Living in La Crescenta, California, has been taking yards aquatic since 2001.

499
00:38:21,501 --> 00:38:30,500
The ecosystem we create with our pond is a community of plants, animals, and microorganisms

500
00:38:30,501 --> 00:38:37,500
that we introduce into the area, and they all depend on each other to survive.

501
00:38:37,501 --> 00:38:42,500
Before the tranquility comes the heavy lifting.

502
00:38:42,501 --> 00:38:46,500
Take this project at a new home in Bel Air, California.

503
00:38:46,501 --> 00:38:50,500
So basically we have about 55 feet of waterfall.

504
00:38:50,501 --> 00:38:52,743
It starts up here with a spring and it's going to come here.

505
00:38:52,744 --> 00:38:54,500
We're going to have a nice cascading waterfall.

506
00:38:54,501 --> 00:38:58,500
We're going to have 10,000 gallons flowing through here, so it's going to be a really nice,

507
00:38:58,501 --> 00:39:02,500
loud, noisy type waterfall. It's going to be very, very beautiful.

508
00:39:02,501 --> 00:39:06,500
The waterfall will feed into a

509
00:39:06,501 --> 00:39:10,500
two-foot deep koi paradise.

510
00:39:10,501 --> 00:39:14,400
First comes a felt underlayment to prevent weeds from invading.

511
00:39:15,540 --> 00:39:18,040
Then a rubber liner will form the bottom of the pond.

512
00:39:18,520 --> 00:39:21,540
Once this is set in place, we set the rocks all the way around.

513
00:39:21,720 --> 00:39:26,220
Starting from the bottom, we rock up and get the different levels rocked in,

514
00:39:26,320 --> 00:39:27,480
and then we pour in gravel.

515
00:39:30,720 --> 00:39:32,600
Just as the dog gets a doghouse,

516
00:39:32,940 --> 00:39:36,420
there's another pond feature that every koi literally can't live without.

517
00:39:36,421 --> 00:39:42,720
So this is basically a koi garage.

518
00:39:42,721 --> 00:39:46,300
In this area we have different predators, there's birds and coyotes and raccoons and

519
00:39:46,301 --> 00:39:47,300
so forth.

520
00:39:47,301 --> 00:39:50,060
So this gives a place for the koi to hide.

521
00:39:50,061 --> 00:39:56,800
And really the water only needs to be about two feet deep for the koi to actually be protected.

522
00:39:56,801 --> 00:40:00,140
The koi aren't expected to do their own housework.

523
00:40:00,141 --> 00:40:02,660
This pond is self-cleaning.

524
00:40:02,661 --> 00:40:07,360
So this is the skimmer, which is basically a mechanical filter.

525
00:40:07,361 --> 00:40:12,660
So we have the water level is about one inch below this line here.

526
00:40:12,661 --> 00:40:15,160
It allows the leaves to be drawn into the basket.

527
00:40:15,161 --> 00:40:19,320
And the leaves are picked up and then you can take out the basket, pull the leaves out

528
00:40:19,321 --> 00:40:21,160
and rinse it out, dump it.

529
00:40:21,161 --> 00:40:28,820
And then we have a filter mat that sits on top of there.

530
00:40:28,821 --> 00:40:34,820
At the top of the falls, a second waterscape filter relies on an unlikely collaborator.

531
00:40:34,821 --> 00:40:39,420
Beneficial bacteria is a very important part of the ecosystem.

532
00:40:39,421 --> 00:40:45,180
It's something that we introduced to the pond to help with controlling the algae.

533
00:40:45,181 --> 00:40:52,000
It's not going to stop the algae growth, but it helps starve the algae of its food source.

534
00:40:52,001 --> 00:40:57,500
The bacteria are introduced at the top of the waterfall in a biofall filter.

535
00:40:57,501 --> 00:41:02,200
The filter contains lava rocks that encourage bacteria to colonize.

536
00:41:02,201 --> 00:41:06,940
The bacteria consume enzymes and other nutrients and minimize the overgrowth of algae in the

537
00:41:06,941 --> 00:41:07,940
ecosystem.

538
00:41:07,941 --> 00:41:13,840
The whole stream actually works as a biological filter as well too, because you're

539
00:41:13,841 --> 00:41:18,980
going to get bacteria that will colonize throughout all the gravel and all the surface space on

540
00:41:18,981 --> 00:41:20,060
the stream.

541
00:41:20,061 --> 00:41:26,220
The koi do their part by feeding along the pond's bottom, dislodging debris that

542
00:41:26,221 --> 00:41:30,880
will end up in the skimmer.

543
00:41:30,881 --> 00:41:35,640
The fish are famously tough and can easily survive freezing temperatures by hibernating

544
00:41:35,641 --> 00:41:39,180
beneath the ice.

545
00:41:39,181 --> 00:41:41,700
This waterscape is complete.

546
00:41:41,701 --> 00:41:46,460
Once the landscaping is finished, this yard will certainly draw a crowd.

547
00:41:46,461 --> 00:41:54,060
A lot of these areas that we put these water features in is a location where maybe they

548
00:41:54,061 --> 00:41:55,380
weren't using very much.

549
00:41:55,381 --> 00:41:57,780
It was just another patch of grass in their lawn.

550
00:41:58,140 --> 00:42:02,740
But now we actually put a living, breathing organism in that area.

551
00:42:06,040 --> 00:42:11,920
Self-sufficient waterscapes are making some rethink our vision of the traditional yard as a carpet of green turf.

552
00:42:15,140 --> 00:42:22,620
That turf requires resources like fertilizer, pesticides, herbicides, and in most cases, a great deal of water.

553
00:42:22,621 --> 00:42:26,440
What is the future of the American yard?

554
00:42:27,200 --> 00:42:33,480
I think that's the struggle as municipalities look to balance the needs of the homeowner

555
00:42:33,481 --> 00:42:35,660
and the desire to have a nice lush lawn,

556
00:42:36,100 --> 00:42:40,060
the desire to use scarce resources appropriately and manage them well.

557
00:42:40,580 --> 00:42:44,340
We have created a savanna from coast to coast.

558
00:42:44,341 --> 00:42:50,480
This whole idea of the lawn around the house has become a national aesthetic.

559
00:42:50,481 --> 00:42:57,780
It's only maybe 150 years old, but this is what people have been taught to believe.

560
00:42:57,781 --> 00:43:05,660
No matter what you cover it with, real grass, fake grass, trees, or water wonderlands,

561
00:43:05,661 --> 00:43:09,240
the yard is a refuge and buffer zone.

562
00:43:09,241 --> 00:43:16,100
It's our chance to create a particular vision of nature right in our own backyard.

